{
  "reasons": {
    "pk-required": "Append-only time series (rate limits, uptime checks, usage) written before this rule; a key needs a table rebuild migration.",
    "pk-not-null": "SQLite lets a TEXT PRIMARY KEY be NULL unless it says NOT NULL. The code always sets ids, and a read-only data check finds no NULL keys; adding NOT NULL means rebuilding each table, done one table at a time.",
    "reference-fk": "Many ids point outside the database (session files, team members on the hub) or at rows that may be removed on purpose; SQLite adds a foreign key only by rebuilding the table.",
    "reference-index": "Not used to look rows up on a growing table today (the full-scan rule would catch that); an index would only slow writes. New lookups must add one.",
    "typed-columns": "Untyped columns of the full-text search tables, which SQLite creates itself.",
    "full-scan": "The chat history cleanup keeps the newest 200 rows: deciding what to drop reads the table, which never holds more than about 200 rows.",
    "list-in-column": "Lists that are only ever read and written whole with their row (files a session touched, packages it used, mentions); nothing queries inside them. Kept on purpose, see docs/adr/0001-lists-and-name-copies.md.",
    "denormalized-copy": "Names of team members and authors live on the team hub, not in this database: the name is kept with the id so it shows without the hub. See docs/adr/0001-lists-and-name-copies.md.",
    "unused-index": "Indexes used by queries built with ${...} (which this check cannot explain) or kept for lookups that are rare but would otherwise read a whole growing table.",
    "select-star": "Rows read whole and mapped to a typed object right after; replacing them is a refactor to do file by file, and no new SELECT * may come in.",
    "n-plus-one": "Loops over a handful of items (the entries of one answer, the projects of one user, the routes of one day); reviewed when the check was added. New ones must use IN or a JOIN.",
    "sql-interpolation": "Reviewed when the check was added: only table names from the schema or a fixed list, quoted identifiers, numbers, or ?-placeholder lists are put into the text; values are always bound with ?.",
    "multi-write": "Found when the rule came in (26): most write in different branches of one method (only one runs), or bump a counter after an insert where a lost bump is harmless. The ones that delete a parent and its children were wrapped in a transaction then; the rest get one when next changed.",
    "await-in-transaction": "None today; a new one fails the gate.",
    "read-await-write": "Found when the rule came in. Each writes a cache or a status keyed by id (UPDATE ... WHERE id = ?): if the row went away meanwhile nothing is written, and two runs at once write the same value. The one that could leave orphan rows and send a false alert (the uptime check) was fixed then: it reads the monitor again after the check."
  },
